 J. Liu, A note on the existence of positive solutions for a class of superlinear elliptic problems, J. Math. Anal. Appl. 392 (2012) 500-506] established the existence of positive solutions for a specific elliptic problem. This paper presents a novel approach to address the same problem. Furthermore, we extend their findings to a more general framework. Our results indicate that the conditions required for the existence of positive solutions in this broader context are less restrictive than those in the original work. Specifically, we demonstrate the existence of positive solutions for the problem:
$$\left\{\begin{array}{ll} -\Delta u = f(u) & \mbox{in } \Omega, \\ u = 0 & \mbox{on } \partial\Omega, \end{array}\right.$$
where $\Omega$ is a bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^N$ with a smooth boundary, and $f$ satisfies the following assumptions:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(A1)] $f \in C^1(\mathbb{R}, \mathbb{R})$, $f(0) = 0$, and $f(t) > 0$ for all $t > 0$;
\item[(A2)] $\displaystyle \lim_{t \to 0^+} \frac{f(t)}{t} = 0$;
\item[(A3)] $\displaystyle \lim_{t \to \infty} \frac{f(t)}{t^{p}} = 0$ for some $p \in (1, 2^*-1]$, where $2^* = \frac{2N}{N-2}$ if $N \geq 3$, and $2^* = \infty$ if $N = 1, 2$;
\item[(A4)] $\displaystyle \lim_{t \to \infty} \frac{t f(t)}{F(t)} = \infty$, where $F(t) = \int_0^t f(s) ds$.
\end{itemize}
Additionally, we prove that the set of solutions is compact. As a consequence, we derive a multiplicity result for the problem. Finally, we provide an example to illustrate the validity of our assumptions.
